# The web/api apps were authored against a flat (yarn-style) node_modules and
# import some transitive deps directly (e.g. @dnd-kit/utilities, @tiptap/core).
# The RN/Expo toolchain in mobile/ has its own, much larger set of phantom
# transitive imports (e.g. @expo/metro-runtime) that only surface as bundler
# errors, not typecheck failures — so this stays a broad hoist rather than a
# hand-enumerated allowlist. The one exclusion: @types/react(-dom), which
# frontend and mobile both declare directly at different versions — hoisting
# it too created a real root-level copy *alongside* each package's own
# pnpm-store-linked copy, and TS treats those as structurally-identical but
# nominally different modules (broke radix-ui's `--radix-*` CSSProperties
# augmentation with "two different types with this name exist" errors).
public-hoist-pattern[]=*
public-hoist-pattern[]=!@types/react
public-hoist-pattern[]=!@types/react-dom
# frontend wants zod@^4 + @hookform/resolvers@^5, mobile wants zod@^3 +
# @hookform/resolvers@^3 — with only one flat hoisted copy of each, the
# resolved pair can mismatch (e.g. hoisted @hookform/resolvers@5 landing next
# to hoisted zod@3): @hookform/resolvers' bundled .d.ts does a bare
# `import * as z4 from 'zod/v4/core'`, which then resolves to the wrong major
# version and breaks frontend/src/components/auth/login-form.tsx's zodResolver
# call with a "_zod.version.minor" structural mismatch. Same class of bug as
# the @types/react exclusion above.
public-hoist-pattern[]=!zod
public-hoist-pattern[]=!@hookform/resolvers
# react-hook-form has no hard "react" dependency, only a broad peer range
# (^16.8 || ^17 || ^18 || ^19) that both frontend (react@19) and mobile
# (react@18) satisfy. Under node-linker=hoisted there's no per-peer instance
# splitting (that's an isolated-linker-only feature) — when one concrete
# react-hook-form version satisfies both workspaces' ranges, pnpm hoists a
# single shared copy with ONE nested node_modules/react to satisfy the peer,
# and Metro/Node resolution finds that nested copy before ever reaching
# root's hoisted react. It picked react@19 (frontend's): mobile's app code
# ran on the root react@18 while react-hook-form's useForm() ran on the
# nested react@19 — two live React copies in one bundle, breaking hooks with
# "Invalid hook call" / "Cannot read property 'useRef' of null" in every
# screen using useForm (e.g. mobile/app/(auth)/login.tsx).
#
# public-hoist-pattern can't fix this on its own the way it does for zod
# below — it only changes what's *hoisted to root*, not whether per-workspace
# copies get created; that still requires a genuine version conflict. The
# actual fix is exact-pinning mobile's react-hook-form (see mobile/package.json)
# to a version strictly below frontend's floor (^7.65.0) — but still >= 7.55.0,
# the peer floor @hookform/resolvers@^5 requires, or `resolver` prop types
# break — so the two workspaces resolve genuinely different versions, forcing
# pnpm to nest separate copies (mirroring how react/react-dom already split
# above). This line is kept for consistency/documentation.
public-hoist-pattern[]=!react-hook-form
# nativewind is only ever depended on by mobile (frontend uses its own
# Tailwind v4 setup directly, no NativeWind involved) — hoisting it to root
# serves no other workspace and just moves where its nested tailwindcss@3
# copy resolves from. Keep it nested under mobile/node_modules alongside
# mobile's own direct tailwindcss@3.4.1 devDependency.
public-hoist-pattern[]=!nativewind

# node-linker=hoisted: mobile's native Android build (CMake/ninja) writes and
# rewrites build artifacts directly under the resolved package directory of
# every native dependency (react-native-*, expo-modules-core, ...). Under the
# default isolated linker, that directory is deep inside the shared
# .pnpm/<name>@<version>_<hash>/node_modules/<name> store — confirmed (by
# building the identical CMakeLists.txt from a short path outside node_modules
# and having it succeed) that ninja cannot reliably converge against that path
# on Windows regardless of long-path support or CMake version, producing
# "manifest 'build.ninja' still dirty after 100 tries". Hoisted linking gives
# every package a real, shallow, non-symlinked node_modules/<name> directory,
# which eliminates the failure.
#
# This also fixes a related but distinct failure: Android Gradle Plugin's
# autolinking registers each native module as an included Gradle project
# pointed at its node_modules/<pkg>/android directory. Under the isolated
# linker that directory is a symlink into the pnpm store, and AGP's variant
# resolution doesn't follow it — every native module project reports
# "No matching variant... No variants exist" and :app:compileDebugJavaWithJavac
# fails to resolve its classpath before Gradle ever reaches the CMake step.
# Hoisted linking's real (non-symlinked) node_modules/<pkg> directories avoid
# this too.
node-linker=hoisted
